## QUESTION : KYA HIGH CPU HAMESHA BURI BAAT HAI ?
========================================================

NAHI.

CPU 80–90% tak normal ho sakta hai.

Kyunki agar application expectedly CPU-intensive hai
(jaise video encoding, data processing, complex calculations),
to high CPU utilization normal hai.

Problem tab hai jab:

    - Response Time badh raha ho
    - Requests queue me wait kar rahi ho
    - Error Rate badh raha ho

Isliye CPU utilization alone decision nahi hota.




==================================================================================
## QUESTION : SCALING KA DECISION LENE SE PEHLE KAUNSE METRICS MONITOR KAROGE ?
==================================================================================

1. JVM CPU USAGE
-----------------

JVM process CPU ka kitna use kar raha hai.

Continuously 90–100% → CPU bottleneck ho sakta hai.

Note: Physical/virtual server ka CPU JVM ko "allocated" nahi hota —
server par chalne wala OS apne saare processes (JVM, DB, OS services)
ke beech share karta hai. Yahan hum JVM process ki CPU consumption
dekh rahe hain.



2. JVM RAM USAGE (HEAP USAGE)
-------------------------------

JVM ko server ki total RAM ka ek fixed portion allocated hoti hai.
Us portion me se ek hissa HEAP ka hota hai.

Heap do values se control hoti hai:

    -Xms  → Initial heap size
    -Xmx  → Maximum heap size

Production me commonly -Xms aur -Xmx same rakhte hain, taaki runtime
me heap grow karne ka overhead na ho.

Heap kyun fill hoti hai?

    More requests → JVM me zyada objects create honge
        - Request / Response Objects
        - Hibernate Entities
        - Session Data
        - JSON Serialization buffers

Heap frequently fill → JVM baar-baar GC run karti hai.
GC ke kuch phases me application threads temporarily pause hote hain
(Stop-The-World pauses) → response time increase.

Agar GC ke baad bhi heap insufficient rahe
    → OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space.

Agar problem heap pressure ki hai, to -Xmx badha sakte hain.
Lekin physical/virtual server ke paas enough RAM honi chahiye.



3. NETWORK UTILIZATION (SERVER NIC)
-------------------------------------

Network bandwidth ek physical/virtual server (NIC) level ka resource hai.
JVM ko koi specific bandwidth allocate nahi hoti.

Application same NIC ka use karti hai jab:

    - Incoming requests aati hain (Client → Application)
    - Outgoing calls jaati hain (Application → DB, Application → Downstream Services)

Agar total traffic NIC ki capacity ke paas pahunch jaye, to packets
queue hone lagte hain → latency increase hoti hai.



4. DATABASE CPU UTILIZATION
-----------------------------

(Jab database alag server par deployed ho.)

Agar DB server ka CPU continuously high utilization par hai,
to query execution time badh jaata hai.

DB Server CPU high hone ke possible reasons:

    - Bahut zyada concurrent queries execute ho rahi hain,
      possibly because DB Connection Pool size bahut zyada
      badha diya gaya hai.

      Zyada concurrent DB connections active hone par DB server
      ko zyada context switching karni padti hai. Isse DB server
      apna significant time context switching me waste kar deta hai,
      actual query instructions execute karne ke bajay.

      Result: DB server CPU utilization high ho jaati hai.

    - Heavy queries (large joins, sorting, aggregation)

    - Missing indexes ki wajah se full table scans

    - Complex calculations / functions queries me

    - Poorly designed queries

Result:

    Query Execution Time increase ho jata hai.

Aur kyunki application database ke response ka wait karti hai,

Overall Application Response Time bhi increase ho jata hai.



5. DATABASE DISK I/O
---------------------

Database apna data disk (SSD/HDD) par store karta hai.

Disk I/O ka matlab hai — database kitni fast disk par read/write
kar pa raha hai.

Agar disk par read/write operations bahut zyada ho jaayein,
to database slow ho sakta hai.

Common reasons high disk I/O ke:

    1. Bahut saare READ operations

       Agar required database page RAM ke Buffer Pool me available
       nahi hai, to database ko wo page disk se RAM me load karna
       padta hai. Kyunki disk RAM se bahut slow hai, overall
       query execution time badh jaata hai.

    2. Bahut saare WRITE operations

       Har write (INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE) ke liye database
       ko WAL / Redo Log disk par persist karna padta hai,
       aur modified data pages ko bhi eventually disk par flush
       karna padta hai. Zyada writes → zyada disk I/O → slow.

    3. Missing indexes on frequently accessed queries

       Index na hone par database full table scan karta hai,
       jisme bahut zyada data pages read karne padte hain.
       Isse disk I/O significantly badh jaata hai.



6. DATABASE CONNECTION POOL USAGE
-----------------------------------

Pool me kitne DB connections busy hain.

Saare busy → requests connection ke liye wait karengi
(Connection Timeout ho sakta hai).


## CONNECTION POOL EXHAUSTION KE COMMON REASONS
-------------------------------------------------

a) SLOW QUERIES
-----------------

   Agar queries bahut slow hain (missing index, large joins,
   poorly designed queries), to DB connections lambi der tak
   occupied rehte hain.

   Agar ek hi time par kai aisi requests aa jaayein jinki
   queries slow hain, to wo saari connections lambi der tak
   occupied rakh deti hain.

   Agar ye situation continuously bani rahe, to pool me
   available free connections kam ho jaate hain aur eventually
   poori DB connection pool exhaust ho jaati hai.


b) CONNECTION LEAK
--------------------

   Application DB connection lekar use close/release nahi karti
   (jaise finally block me connection.close() missing).

   Result:

       Pool me free connections gradually kam hote jaate hain
            ↓
       Eventually saare connections borrowed state me atak jaate hain
            ↓
       Pool me koi available connection nahi bachta
            ↓
       Naye requests configured connection-timeout tak wait karte hain
            ↓
       Timeout cross hone par request FAIL ho jaati hai
       (Connection Timeout)



7. THREAD POOL USAGE
---------------------

   Kitne Tomcat worker threads currently busy hain.

   Saare busy → naye requests queue me wait karti hain, aur
   configured timeout ke baad wo requests fail ho jaati hain.

   Important:

   Worker threads badhane se pehle ye find out karna zaroori hai
   ki threads itni der tak occupied kyun hain:

       a) Kya genuinely high traffic volume ki wajah se hai?
          → Is case me thread pool badhana valid ho sakta hai.

       b) Ya koi blocking operation hai?
          (jaise slow DB queries, slow downstream API calls, network latency , lock wait)
          → Is case me sirf thread pool badhane se problem
            aur badh sakti hai, kyunki wo bhi usi slow resource
            ka wait karengi.

   Isliye pehle root cause identify karo, phir decide karo
   ki thread pool badhana hai ya actual bottleneck fix karna hai.



8. REQUEST RATE (RPS)
-----------------------

Ek second me application par kitni requests aa rahi hain.

Ye batata hai ki traffic increase ho raha hai ya nahi.



9. RESPONSE TIME
------------------

Ek request ko complete hone me kitna time lag raha hai.

Response time badh raha hai → system slow ho raha hai.

Average alone misleading hota hai (slow requests chhup jaati hain).
Isliye P95/P99 dekhte hain.


- P95 LATENCY
--------------

95% requests is time ke andar complete ho rahi hain.

Majority users ka experience.


- P99 LATENCY
---------------

99% requests is time ke andar complete ho rahi hain.

Worst-case user experience.


## EXAMPLE
-----------

100 requests:

    95 requests → 100 ms
    4 requests  → 500 ms
    1 request   → 5000 ms

    Average = 165 ms   (normal lagta hai, lekin misleading)
    P95     = 100 ms
    P99     = 500 ms
    Max     = 5000 ms



10. ERROR RATE
---------------

Kitni requests fail ho rahi hain (4xx / 5xx).

Error rate increase → system health ke liye warning sign.


